      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Given $X∼U\\{-10,-9,-8,…,10\\},$ compute $P(-3<X\\le 6).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-10,-9,-8,…,10\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-10,-9,-8,…,10\\}$ has $|S|=10-(-10)+1=21$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}, & x=-10,-9,-8,…,10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $-3<X\\le 6$ are $-2,-1,0,…,6.$ There are $6-(-2)+1=9$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(-3<X\\le 6)=9\\cdot \\frac{1}{21}=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
